05:08.000 --> 05:12.000
The last topic here, I'm not going to talk about very much.

05:12.000 --> 05:14.000
I just want to give you a heads up.

05:14.000 --> 05:18.000
Unlike in other languages, in Python, you can have multiple inheritance.

05:18.000 --> 05:23.000
So you can inherit from multiple different other classes at once.

05:24.000 --> 05:31.000
And things get a bit hairy, but one use case of this is something called a mixing.

05:31.000 --> 05:38.000
So a mixing might be a class that just has a method like this on it.

05:38.000 --> 05:42.000
And I don't care if my 4 by 4 is eco friendly.

05:42.000 --> 05:45.000
I'm driving it through the grass and mowing it down.

05:45.000 --> 05:50.000
And what do I care if it's, you know, gas or solar?

05:51.000 --> 05:55.000
So where I, in the cases where I do care maybe like a car,

05:55.000 --> 06:01.000
I can have an additional class that I inherit from that just has this method.

06:01.000 --> 06:05.000
So one of my favorite, one common use case for this,

06:05.000 --> 06:10.000
you might see if you're declaring database tables in Python.

06:10.000 --> 06:13.000
You might have some mix-ins for something like a date column,

06:13.000 --> 06:17.000
where you don't necessarily want, you want to have a date,

06:17.000 --> 06:23.000
a created on date on all of your objects, just, you know, for compliance reasons.

06:23.000 --> 06:27.000
But you don't want to have to say, you know, class vehicle dot date equals whatever,

06:27.000 --> 06:30.000
class motor cycle dot date equals whatever.

06:30.000 --> 06:35.000
So you can just have a mix-in class that will create that column for you.

06:35.000 --> 06:38.000
And provide all of the methods that you might need to use it.

06:38.000 --> 06:42.000
I've seen Python code that on an objects that don't,

06:42.000 --> 06:45.000
or classes that don't inherit from anything else,

06:45.000 --> 06:49.000
but the object keyword is the object that they inherit from,

06:49.000 --> 06:52.000
instead of just saying the name of the class column,

06:52.000 --> 06:56.000
they'll have object after the class definition.

06:56.000 --> 07:02.000
So that is, yeah, that is one of the differences between Python 2 and Python 3.

07:02.000 --> 07:06.000
In Python 3, you don't need to do that anymore.

07:06.000 --> 07:08.000
It's redundant, you know,

07:08.000 --> 07:11.000
simple as better than complex.

07:11.000 --> 07:15.000
You can still do it, and it's not a syntax error.

07:15.000 --> 07:18.000
You just shouldn't.